00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.820
Mind Your Decisions, I'm Presh Talwalkar

00:00:03.759 --> 00:00:06.059
Consider the numbers 1, 2, 3, &amp; 4.

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:13.439
There are six possible products that can be made by pairing these numbers. These will be illustrated below.

00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:20.189
Now to explain the puzzle. I'm thinking of four positive numbers.

00:00:20.859 --> 00:00:24.599
There are six possible products from pairing the numbers.

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:30.150
I will reveal five of the products have values of two, three, four, five and six.

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:33.599
What is the value of the sixth product?

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:40.139
Pause if you'd like to give this problem a try and when you're ready keep watching to learn how to solve this problem.

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:58.809
Before I get to the solution, I'll admit this was a tricky puzzle and I did not solve it myself.

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:01.690
But I thought the solution was very clever.

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:05.680
So let's go back to our original example of 1, 2, 3 &amp; 4.

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:11.410
The trick is to consider the product of 1, 2, 3 &amp; 4 which is equal to 24.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:15.849
Now, let's look at the product from pairing these numbers.

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:18.550
Let's exchange these two pairs.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:26.139
Look in the very first line. If we take the product of these pairs, we have 2 times 12.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.140
Which is equal to 24.

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:30.580
This is also true in the second line,

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:34.989
We have 3 times 8 which is equal to 24. And in the very last line

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:42.910
We have 4 times 6 which is equal to 24. So we found three different ways to get the product of the four original numbers.

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:49.509
So suppose the numbers are a, b, c, d and we only know the product of the pairs.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:57.549
So we have four unknown numbers, but we can still make the product a, b, c, d in three different ways.

00:01:58.030 --> 00:01:59.930
That's the key insight.

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:02.860
Furthermore, suppose bc is the unknown pair.

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:10.479
Well, if we write that as X, we still have two different ways that we can get the product a, b, c, d. And,

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:18.579
the other pair ad which is not listed will pair with the unknown product to get

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:20.480
abcd. So

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:26.200
Now, let's solve our problem. We know that the pairs are equal to 2, 3, 4, 5 &amp; 6.

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:29.590
So we need to consider,

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:34.000
pairing each of these and then examining the product of each of these pairs.

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:40.089
We're hoping to get two values that are equal. Because that will be the product of the four original numbers.

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:45.160
When we examine these products, we have two ways that we can make 12.

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:51.189
So this will reveal that 12 is the product of the four original numbers.

00:02:51.799 --> 00:02:58.509
We have 2 times 6 2 which is 12, 3 times 4 which is 12. And so 5 is the product which is not involved.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:01.120
Therefore five times x,

00:03:01.550 --> 00:03:07.339
must be equal to 12. And that six product will be equal to 12 over five or

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:10.040
2.4 . And that's our answer.

00:03:11.160 --> 00:03:15.529
Now for a challenge, what could the four original numbers be?

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:24.410
Thanks for making mind your decisions one of the best channels on YouTube. As always, thanks for watching and thanks for your support

